  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "**This is the easy version of the problem. Here, you need to print min⁡(R,3)\\min(R, 3)min(R,3) instead of RRR itself.**\n\nYou are given an integer NNN.\n\nAn array AAA is called good if it satisfies all of the following conditions:\n\n\n1≤Ai≤N1 \\le A_i \\le N1≤Ai​≤N for all 1≤i≤∣A∣1 \\le i \\le |A|1≤i≤∣A∣\nAi≤Ai+1A_i \\le A_{i + 1}Ai​≤Ai+1​ for all 1≤i&lt;∣A∣1 \\le i &lt; |A|1≤i&lt;∣A∣\nA1+A2+…+A∣A∣=NA_1 + A_2 + \\ldots + A_{|A|} = NA1​+A2​+…+A∣A∣​=N\nA1×A2×…×A∣A∣=NA_1 \\times A_2 \\times \\ldots \\times A_{|A|} = NA1​×A2​×…×A∣A∣​=N\nIn other words, the array should consist of non-decreasing positive integers and its product and sum both must be NNN.\n\nHere, ∣A∣|A|∣A∣ denotes the length of AAA.\n\nLet RRR denote the number of good arrays. Your task is to find the value min⁡(R,3)\\min(R, 3)min(R,3), i.e. you should be able to determine whether R=0,1,2R = 0, 1, 2R=0,1,2 or ≥3\\ge 3≥3.\n\nFor example, for N=6N = 6N=6, R=2R = 2R=2 because there are 222 good arrays, [6][6][6] and [1,2,3][1, 2, 3][1,2,3].\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of a single line of input, containing a single integer NNN.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the value min⁡(R,3)\\min(R, 3)min(R,3).",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤201 \\le T \\le 201≤T≤20",
      "1≤N≤1091 \\le N \\le 10^91≤N≤109"
    ],
